**Read:** [Outgassing properties of vacuum materials — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT), coupled gas/surface balances and accumulation-method limits; [Total and Partial Pressure Measurement — Karl Jousten](../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M), measurement effects.

A molecule released from a wall can be captured again before reaching the pump. In a low-coverage, well-mixed model, let ng and ns be gas and surface amounts. Write dng/dt=ṅfeed−(S/V)ng−ka ng+kd ns and dns/dt=ka ng−kd ns. The total changes only by external feed and pump removal.

This model uses constant linear exchange coefficients and no site saturation. With no pump or feed, equilibrium gives ns/ng=ka/kd while conserving total amount. With pumping, observed pressure can have two relaxation scales. A slow trace therefore need not be the intrinsic desorption time of an isolated molecule.

**Work:** Derive the combined inventory and the two decay rates. Examine fast-adsorption and fast-pumping limits. Compare pressure traces with and without a surface population and identify which additional measurement could distinguish hidden storage from a changed pumping speed. State where finite site capacity requires a nonlinear extension.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
